Switching to Google Drive for distributing your beta is a great move. To sync a specific folder without having to drag it inside the main "Google Drive" folder, you will use the Google Drive for Desktop app.

Here is exactly how to set it up so you can right-click and share your compiled .exe directly from your current workspace.

1. Add the Project Folder to Drive
Click the Google Drive icon in your Windows System Tray (down by the clock).

Click the Gear icon (Settings) in the top right of the Drive menu and select Preferences.

In the left sidebar, click My Computer (or "My Laptop/PC").

Click the Add folder button.

Navigate to your project directory, select it, and click Select Folder.

Check the box for Sync with Google Drive and hit Done, then Save.

Google Drive will now silently back up that entire directory in the background.

2. Get the Share Link for the .exe
Once the folder is syncing, you don't even need to open your browser to share it with your beta testers.

Open your standard Windows File Explorer and go to your project folder.

Right-click your compiled .exe file.

Look for the Google Drive logo in the context menu and select Share (or "Copy link to clipboard").

Under "General access," change it from "Restricted" to Anyone with the link.

Copy that link and send it to your testers! Whenever you compile a new version over that same file, Drive will automatically update it in the cloud, and their link will still work.

⚠️ Important Developer Warning: File Locks
Because you are actively developing in this folder, you need to watch out for File Locking.

When you run go build to create a fresh .exe, Go needs to delete the old one and write the new one. If Google Drive happens to be in the middle of uploading or scanning that .exe at the exact millisecond you hit Enter, Windows will throw an "Access Denied" error, and your compile will fail.

The Workaround:
If you start getting "Access Denied" errors while coding, the easiest fix is to create a dedicated subfolder (e.g., \Release_Builds\) inside your main project directory.

Tell Google Drive to sync only the Release_Builds folder instead of the entire project root.

When you have a version ready for your testers, just copy/paste the finished .exe into that folder. It keeps your live coding environment completely safe from cloud-sync interference!
